-- Q612: Financial Statement Analysis [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It includes inventory and receivables in its numerator alongside cash","It uses only cash and cash equivalents divided by current liabilities","It accounts for both current and long-term debt obligations","It measures operating profitability rather than short-term liquidity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per ASC 305-10, the cash ratio restricts the numerator to cash and cash equivalents divided by current liabilities, making it the most conservative liquidity measure. It excludes receivables and inventory that carry collection or liquidation risk.
Wrong (A): Including inventory describes the current ratio, not the cash ratio.
Wrong (C): The cash ratio measures short-term liquidity only and does not incorporate long-term debt.
Wrong (D): The cash ratio is a liquidity metric, not a profitability metric.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 612;

-- Q617: Prospective Analysis and Forecasting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Model A forecasts $212,000 and Model B forecasts $228,000; the higher alpha places more weight on recent actuals and is preferred when data reflects a genuine trend","Both models produce identical forecasts of $220,000 because alpha only affects forecasts three or more periods ahead from the starting point","Model A forecasts $228,000 and Model B forecasts $212,000 because a lower alpha reacts more aggressively to recent data than a higher alpha","Model A forecasts $240,000 and Model B forecasts $200,000 because each model uses only the forecast or the actual value, never both"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AT Section 301, exponential smoothing calculates F(t+1) = alpha x Actual + (1 - alpha) x Forecast. Model A: 0.3 x $240,000 + 0.7 x $200,000 = $212,000. Model B: 0.7 x $240,000 + 0.3 x $200,000 = $228,000. A higher alpha weights recent actuals more heavily.
Wrong (B): Different alphas always produce different forecasts; alpha affects every period immediately.
Wrong (C): This reverses the alpha-weight relationship; higher alpha is more responsive, not lower.
Wrong (D): Both models always blend the prior forecast with the actual observation; neither ignores an input.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 617;

-- Q619: Prospective Analysis and Forecasting [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The prediction is reliable because an R-squared of 0.88 means 88% of future predictions will fall within the confidence interval regardless of input","Extrapolation improves accuracy because the model becomes more precise as the independent variable increases beyond the observed sample range","R-squared of 0.88 measures in-sample fit only; at 15,000 hours the linear cost relationship may break down due to overtime, capacity, or inefficiency effects","The model is invalid at any point because an R-squared below 1.00 means the regression equation has no meaningful predictive value"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 270-10 cost estimation principles, R-squared of 0.88 indicates 88% of variation in production costs is explained by machine hours within the observed 2,000-8,000 hour range. At 15,000 hours, nearly double the maximum observed, the cost structure may change nonlinearly. R-squared is an in-sample statistic, not a guarantee of out-of-range accuracy.
Wrong (A): R-squared measures historical fit, not the accuracy of predictions outside the observed data range.
Wrong (B): Extrapolation degrades reliability because the model has no data to validate assumptions beyond 8,000 hours.
Wrong (D): An R-squared of 0.88 is strong; it does not need to be 1.00 to have predictive value.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 619;

-- Q621: Prospective Analysis and Forecasting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Changes multiple variables simultaneously to model combined effects on forecasted outcomes","Changes one variable at a time while holding all other assumptions constant","Always produces a single point estimate rather than a range of possible outcomes","Is only applicable to capital budgeting decisions and not to financial forecasts"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT Section 301, sensitivity analysis isolates the impact of a single variable by changing one input at a time while holding others constant. This identifies which individual assumptions drive the greatest variation in projected results, unlike scenario analysis which changes multiple variables simultaneously.
Wrong (A): Changing multiple variables simultaneously describes scenario analysis, not sensitivity analysis.
Wrong (C): Sensitivity analysis produces multiple estimates, one per variable tested, not a single point estimate.
Wrong (D): Sensitivity analysis applies broadly to financial forecasting, not exclusively to capital budgeting.$EXPL$
