-- Q640: Financial Valuation Methods [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["FCFF decreases from $12M to $8M because the $4M capex increase directly reduces free cash flow, and capital-intensive firms trade at lower EV/EBITDA multiples","FCFF is unaffected because capital expenditures are excluded from the free cash flow calculation and appear only on the balance sheet","FCFF increases because higher capex generates proportionally higher revenue immediately, and capital-intensive firms trade at premium multiples","FCFF decreases but enterprise value increases because the market rewards companies that invest heavily in growth regardless of cash flow"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the FCFF framework consistent with ASC 820, FCFF equals operating cash flow minus capital expenditures. Increasing capex from $8M to $12M reduces FCFF from $12M to $8M. Capital-intensive companies trade at lower EV/EBITDA multiples because higher capex means less of each dollar of EBITDA converts to free cash flow available to investors.
Wrong (B): Capex is a direct deduction in the FCFF formula, not excluded.
Wrong (C): Capital-intensive firms generally trade at lower multiples, and capex does not generate immediate proportional revenue.
Wrong (D): Lower free cash flow typically reduces enterprise value in DCF models; the market does not automatically reward heavy investment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 640;

-- Q641: Financial Valuation Methods [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The value increases from $40 to $42, a modest 5% increase proportional to the 1-point growth change, reflecting the model''s linear sensitivity","The value increases from $40 to $50, a 25% increase, because the denominator (k minus g) shrinks from 5% to 4%, amplifying value hyperbolically","The value decreases because a higher growth rate increases risk, which the model penalizes through an implicit higher discount rate","The value is unchanged because the Gordon Growth Model only responds to changes in the discount rate, not the growth rate"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the Gordon Growth Model consistent with ASC 820-10-35, V = D1 / (k - g). At g = 7% and k = 12%, the denominator is 5%, implying D1 = $2.00. At g = 8%, the denominator becomes 4%, and V rises to $50. The hyperbolic relationship means small changes in g produce disproportionately large value changes as g approaches k.
Wrong (A): The relationship is hyperbolic, not linear; a 1-point change produces a 25% increase, not 5%.
Wrong (C): Growth rate and discount rate are independent inputs; higher growth increases value, not risk.
Wrong (D): The model responds to changes in both the discount rate and the growth rate.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 641;

-- Q643: Financial Valuation Methods [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["P/E is preferable because net income reflects actual returns available to equity holders after all obligations, making it more conservative","EV/EBITDA is preferable because it neutralizes capital structure and tax policy differences, providing a comparable operating performance basis","EV/EBITDA is preferable only because it produces a lower number, making targets appear cheaper and easier to justify to the board","Both metrics are equally valid because identical net income means the companies are equally profitable regardless of leverage or tax rates"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per ASC 820, EV/EBITDA neutralizes financing and tax distortions. Enterprise value captures total firm value regardless of financing mix, and EBITDA measures operating performance before interest, taxes, depreciation, and amortization. Ironclad''s 70% debt creates interest expense that depresses net income, inflating P/E, while Freeport''s tax credits inflate net income, deflating P/E.
Wrong (A): P/E''s sensitivity to financing and tax differences makes it less comparable across different capital structures.
Wrong (C): The metric''s value lies in comparability, not in producing a lower number.
Wrong (D): Identical net income does not mean equal operating profitability when leverage and tax rates differ substantially.$EXPL$
